const e=[{id:"sv-1-441",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which description matches the course book’s glossary definition of the **Six Sigma system**?",options:["A single statistical measure equal to 3.4 defects per million opportunities, reported to customers each month","A quality standard that guarantees zero defects once a process has been audited and certified","A production system that removes waste by making only what downstream demand pulls through the process","A proven set of analytical tools, project management, reporting and management techniques combined into one problem-solving and improvement method"],answer:3,hint:"The glossary describes Six Sigma as a *combination* of tools and techniques forming one method, not as a single number.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book glossary (p. 589) calls the Six Sigma system “a proven set of analytical tools, project management techniques, reporting methods and management techniques combined to form a powerful problem solving and business improvement methodology”."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A single DPMO figure is only the *metric* meaning. “Zero defects” is Crosby’s slogan. Making only what is pulled describes Lean (Toyota Production System)."}],final:"Six Sigma system = combined tools and techniques forming one improvement method → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — 3.4 DPMO is only the metric meaning of Six Sigma, not the whole system the glossary defines.","Incorrect — zero defects is Philip Crosby’s slogan; Six Sigma accepts a very small, non-zero defect rate.","Incorrect — producing only what demand pulls describes Lean and the Toyota Production System, not the Six Sigma system.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 589"},{id:"sv-1-442",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"The book moves every project from a practical problem to a **statistical problem**. What is a statistical problem, according to the book’s glossary?",options:["A problem whose cause is already known to the process owner and only needs approval","A problem caused by an error in a formula or report produced by the team","A problem that is addressed with facts and data analysis methods","A problem that affects only the process variance and never the process mean"],answer:2,hint:"Glossary wording: a statistical problem is tackled with “facts and data analysis methods”.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 589) defines a statistical problem as “a problem that is addressed with facts and data analysis methods”. It is the second box of the practical → statistical → statistical solution → practical solution path."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A known cause awaiting approval is solution jumping, not a statistical problem. A spreadsheet error is a data-quality issue. A statistical problem can concern the mean, the spread or both."}],final:"Statistical problem = addressed with facts and data analysis → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a cause already “known” without data is an opinion; Six Sigma turns it into a data question first.","Incorrect — a formula or report error is a data-quality slip, not the book’s meaning of a statistical problem.","","Incorrect — statistical problems can involve the mean, the spread or both; the book’s mission is to move the mean and/or reduce σ."],bookRef:"Book p. 589"},{id:"sv-1-443",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Typical project goals are to reduce cycle time, errors or cost. In **statistical terms**, what does the book say these goals translate into?",options:["Moving the process mean and/or reducing the process standard deviation","Widening the specification limits until all output conforms","Increasing the sample size of every routine inspection","Setting the process mean equal to the upper specification limit"],answer:0,hint:"Two levers on a distribution: where it sits (mean) and how wide it is (standard deviation).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s “Mission” page (p. 270) says project goals translate statistically into the need to **move the process mean and/or reduce the process standard deviation**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Wider limits change the customer requirement, not the process. Bigger samples improve estimates, not performance. A mean sitting on the USL would put half the output out of specification."}],final:"Statistical mission = move the mean and/or reduce σ → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — widening limits changes the requirement; customers rarely accept it and the process itself is not improved.","Incorrect — larger samples improve the precision of estimates but do not change the process mean or spread.","Incorrect — a mean on the upper specification limit would put about half of the output outside the limit."],bookRef:"Book p. 270"},{id:"sv-1-444",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"How is “**Thinking the Six Sigma Way**” (TSSW), as described in the book’s glossary, applied to everyday problems?",options:["By adjusting the process every time the output moves up or down","By setting a target for the output and rewarding staff who reach it","By inspecting every output against its specification before it is released","By viewing outcomes as the result of cause-and-effect relationships and using Six Sigma concepts to solve them"],answer:3,hint:"TSSW is a mental model built on cause and effect, the same logic as Y = f(X).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary (p. 589) defines TSSW as “a mental model for improvement which perceives outcomes through a cause and effect relationship combined with six sigma concepts”."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Reacting to every movement is tampering. Targets and rewards push on the output only. Inspection sorts results but does not touch causes."}],final:"TSSW = see outcomes as cause and effect, then apply Six Sigma concepts → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — reacting to every movement is tampering with common-cause variation, which usually increases variation.","Incorrect — rewarding the output pushes on Y only; TSSW looks for the causes that drive the output.","Incorrect — inspection detects defects after they occur; it does not reason about their causes.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 589"},{id:"sv-1-445",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"According to the book’s glossary, what does the **sigma score (Z)** of a process represent?",options:["The proportion of output that falls between the process mean and the target value","The number of long-term standard deviations between the lower and upper specification limits","The number of short-term standard deviations between the process centre and the closest specification limit","The number of defects per million opportunities after the 1.5σ shift has been added"],answer:2,hint:"Z is a *count of standard deviations*, measured from the centre to the **nearest** limit.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary (p. 588) calls Z “a commonly used measure of process capability… the number of short-term standard deviations between the center of the process and the closest specification limit”, also called the sigma level."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Two words decide it: **short-term** standard deviations and the **closest** limit."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A proportion is a probability, not Z. The distance between both limits describes the specification width (used in Cp). DPMO is a defect rate, a different way to express capability."}],final:"Z = short-term σ’s from centre to the closest spec limit → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a proportion of output is a probability; Z counts standard deviations, it is not a share of the data.","Incorrect — the full specification width between both limits is the idea behind Cp, not the sigma score.","","Incorrect — DPMO is a defect rate; the book lists it as a separate way to express capability."],trap:"Z is not “the proportion of data between X and μ” — it is a number of standard deviations.",bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-446",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"A Green Belt finds that an output is both **not capable** and **not centred**. According to the book, what should usually be addressed **first**, and why?",options:["The mean, because shifting the mean is always the primary objective of a project","The specification limits, because customers prefer wider limits to process changes","The sample size, because off-centre data cannot be used for capability","The variation, because high variation creates high uncertainty"],answer:3,hint:"The book: when a process is incapable *and* off-centre, “most of the time” minimise and control variation first.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 209) says that in this case you must usually minimise and get control of the **variation** first, “because high variation creates high uncertainty”. The exception is a simple adjustment that shifts the average."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The *primary objective* in the book is shifting the mean **and** reducing variation together, not the mean alone. Customers usually reject wider limits. Off-centre data can still be analysed for capability."}],final:"Incapable and off-centre → reduce and control variation first → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book’s primary objective is shifting the mean and reducing variation together, not the mean on its own.","Incorrect — the book notes customers usually do not accept wider specification limits.","Incorrect — off-centre data are still valid for capability; centring is part of what capability measures.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 209"},{id:"sv-1-447",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"A process performing at Six Sigma produces about **3.4 defects per million** of what?",options:["Customers served","Defective units inspected","Opportunities for a defect","Units produced"],answer:2,hint:"Read the acronym: DPMO = defects per million **opportunities**.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Six Sigma metric is **3.4 DPMO** — defects per million *opportunities*. A unit can have several opportunities for a defect."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Per million units would be DPM (parts per million). Customers and defective units are different denominators."}],final:"Six Sigma = 3.4 defects per million opportunities → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — counting per customer is a different denominator; Six Sigma’s figure is per opportunity.","Incorrect — defective units are counted with yield or proportion defective, not with DPMO.","","Incorrect — per million units is DPM (parts per million); one unit can contain several opportunities."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.1"},{id:"sv-1-448",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement about **variation** is CORRECT, according to the book?",options:["Variation exists in everything; it can be minimised and controlled but not eliminated","Variation can be removed completely once every special cause has been found","Variation matters only when the process mean is away from its target","Variation is reduced most easily by widening the specification limits"],answer:0,hint:"The book: “variation exists in everything… you cannot eliminate it completely”.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 209) says variation exists in everything; you cannot eliminate it completely, but you **can minimise and control** it."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Removing special causes still leaves common-cause variation. A centred process can still produce defects if its spread is wide. Wider limits change the requirement, not the variation."}],final:"Variation can be minimised and controlled, never eliminated → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — removing special causes leaves common-cause variation, which is always present.","Incorrect — a centred process with a wide spread still produces defects, so variation always matters.","Incorrect — widening the limits changes the customer requirement; the process variation stays the same."],bookRef:"Book p. 209"},{id:"sv-1-449",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about **short-term** and **long-term** data is CORRECT?",options:["Long-term data excludes between-subgroup effects such as shift changes and new material lots","Data collected on a very large volume of output in one day is long-term data","Most processes provide purely short-term or purely long-term data, never a mix","Short-term data represents the entitlement situation, with controllable sources of variation held steady"],answer:3,hint:"Short-term = the process at its best (entitlement). Long-term = includes all the variation expected over time.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 214) says short-term data “basically represent your entitlement situation”, while long-term data includes, in theory, all the variation you can expect. You never have purely one or the other."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Between-subgroup effects are exactly what long-term data adds. The book warns not to be misled by volume: 100,000 units in one day is still short-term (p. 213)."}],final:"Short-term data = entitlement (best demonstrated) situation → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — long-term data includes between-subgroup variation such as shifts and lots; that is why its sigma is lower.","Incorrect — the book warns against being misled by volume; one day of output is short-term, however large.","Incorrect — the book says you never have purely short-term or purely long-term data.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 213–214"},{id:"sv-1-450",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"An invoice contains a wrong address, a wrong tariff and a missing VAT line. Which statement is CORRECT?",options:["The invoice is one defect and three defectives","The invoice is not defective until a fourth error is found","The invoice is three defects but only one defective unit","Defects and defectives are the same count, so it is three of each"],answer:2,hint:"Defect = each failure to meet a requirement. Defective = a unit with at least one defect.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **defect** is any single failure to meet a requirement; a **defective** is a unit that has at least one defect."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Three separate errors = three defects. They all sit on one invoice = one defective unit."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Reversing the words, waiting for more errors or treating both counts as equal all break the definitions."}],final:"Three errors on one invoice = 3 defects, 1 defective → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this reverses the terms: each error is a defect, and the invoice is the defective unit.","Incorrect — one defect is enough to make a unit defective; there is no minimum number of errors.","","Incorrect — defects count failures and defectives count units, so the two counts usually differ."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.1"},{id:"sv-1-451",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about Six Sigma as a **metric** are CORRECT?

1. Six Sigma performance corresponds to about 3.4 defects per million opportunities.
2. The 3.4 DPMO figure includes an allowance of about 1.5σ for long-term drift.
3. A higher sigma level means a wider process spread for the same limits.
4. The sigma level is counted from the mean to the furthest specification limit.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"Check each statement against: 3.4 DPMO, 1.5σ shift, smaller spread = higher sigma, nearest limit.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 is true (Six Sigma = 3.4 DPMO). 2 is true (the figure already includes the 1.5σ long-term shift). 3 is false: a higher sigma level means a **narrower** spread. 4 is false: Z uses the **nearest** limit."},{title:"Match a pair",body:"Only statements 1 and 2 are true, which is the first house pair."}],final:"True statements are 1 and 2 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — includes statement 3; a higher sigma level means a narrower spread, not a wider one.","Incorrect — both are false: 3 reverses the spread, and 4 should say the nearest limit.","Incorrect — includes statement 4; the sigma level is counted to the nearest specification limit."],bookRef:"Book pp. 214, 588"},{id:"sv-1-452",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"statements",difficulty:3,stem:`An output misses its specification limits. Which statements match the book’s **problem-solving options**?

1. Reducing variation is usually the easiest option.
2. Shifting the mean is usually the easiest option.
3. Shifting the mean and reducing variation together is the primary objective of Six Sigma projects.
4. Variation can be eliminated completely by shifting the mean.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:1,hint:"Book pp. 209–210: shift the mean (easiest), reduce variation (harder), both together (primary objective).",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 is false — the book says reducing variation is “typically not so easy”. 2 is true — shifting the mean is “usually the easiest option”. 3 is true — doing both is “the primary objective of Six Sigma projects”. 4 is false — moving the mean does not change spread, and variation can never be eliminated."},{title:"Match a pair",body:"The true statements are 2 and 3."}],final:"True statements are 2 and 3 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — includes statement 1; the book says reducing variation is typically not so easy.","","Incorrect — includes statement 4; shifting the mean leaves the spread unchanged and variation is never fully eliminated.","Incorrect — both are false: reducing variation is the harder option, and variation cannot be eliminated."],bookRef:"Book pp. 209–210"},{id:"sv-1-453",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word(s) in the following sentence.

In Six Sigma, the Greek letter σ represents the [?] of a process output.`,options:["mean","median","range","standard deviation"],answer:3,hint:"σ measures how far results typically sit from their average.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"σ (sigma) is the **standard deviation** — the typical distance of results from the mean. The *sigma level* then counts how many σ’s fit between the mean and the nearest limit."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The mean and median describe the centre. The range is a simpler spread measure (largest minus smallest), shown as R, not σ."}],final:"σ = standard deviation → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the mean is the centre of the data, usually written μ or x̄, not σ.","Incorrect — the median is the middle value, a measure of centre, not of spread.","Incorrect — the range is the largest minus the smallest value, written R, not σ.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-454",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

The book states: “We have learned the heart and soul of Six Sigma is [?].”`,options:["customers","data","leadership","teamwork"],answer:1,hint:"Six Sigma replaces opinion with evidence at every step.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 172) says “the heart and soul of Six Sigma is **data**”. Every phase turns opinions into facts."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Customers, leadership and teamwork all matter, but the quoted sentence names data."}],final:"Heart and soul of Six Sigma = data → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — customers define requirements, but the quoted sentence names data as the heart of Six Sigma.","","Incorrect — leadership sustains the programme (the philosophy meaning), but the quote names data.","Incorrect — teamwork supports projects, but the book’s quote names data."],bookRef:"Book p. 172"},{id:"sv-1-455",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"The book lists several ways to express process capability. Which is NOT one of them?",options:["A percentage yield","A pair of control limits","A defect rate such as DPMO","A sigma score (Z)"],answer:1,hint:"Capability compares output with the **specification**; control limits describe stability, not capability.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary (p. 584) says capability can be expressed as a yield percentage, a defect rate (dpm, dpmo), an index (Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk) or a sigma score (Z)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Control limits come from the process data and show whether the process is **stable**. They say nothing about meeting the customer’s limits."}],final:"Control limits measure stability, not capability → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — yield is listed by the book as a way to express capability.","","Incorrect — defect rates such as DPMO are listed as a way to express capability.","Incorrect — the sigma score Z is listed as a way to express capability."],trap:"Stable is not the same as capable: control limits are the voice of the process.",bookRef:"Book p. 584"},{id:"sv-1-456",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"Which is NOT a meaning of the term “Six Sigma”?",options:["A guarantee that no defect will ever reach a customer","A metric of process performance","A DMAIC problem-solving methodology","A data-driven management philosophy"],answer:0,hint:"Three meanings: metric, methodology, philosophy. None of them promises zero defects.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Six Sigma is a **metric** (3.4 DPMO), a **methodology** (DMAIC) and a **management philosophy** (data-based decisions, continuous improvement)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Six Sigma accepts a very small, non-zero defect rate. A zero-defect guarantee is not one of its meanings (and “zero defects” is Crosby’s slogan)."}],final:"A zero-defect guarantee is not a meaning of Six Sigma → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — Six Sigma as a metric (sigma level, 3.4 DPMO) is one of its three meanings.","Incorrect — DMAIC is the Six Sigma methodology, one of its three meanings.","Incorrect — the management philosophy is the third meaning of Six Sigma."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.1"},{id:"sv-1-457",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A fibre-repair team narrows the spread of its repair times. The mean and the customer’s SLA limit stay exactly the same. What happens to the **sigma level**?",options:["It decreases, because a smaller standard deviation lowers the sigma level","It stays the same, because the SLA limit did not move","It cannot change unless the mean moves closer to the target","It increases, because more standard deviations now fit between the mean and the limit"],answer:3,hint:"Same distance to the limit, smaller σ → more σ’s fit in that distance.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The sigma level counts how many standard deviations fit between the mean and the nearest limit. The picture to remember: **the limit stays still, the curve gets narrower**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The distance from mean to SLA is unchanged, but each σ is now smaller, so more of them fit — the sigma level rises and defects fall."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Confusing σ (spread) with sigma level gives the “decreases” answer. Z depends on both the distance and σ, so it changes even when the limit and mean do not."}],final:"Smaller σ, same distance → higher sigma level → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this confuses σ (the spread) with the sigma level; a smaller σ gives a higher sigma level.","Incorrect — the sigma level depends on σ as well as the limit, so it changes when the spread changes.","Incorrect — reducing variation alone raises the sigma level; moving the mean is not required.",""],trap:"Do not confuse σ (spread) with the sigma level: larger σ = lower sigma level.",bookRef:"Book pp. 209, 588"},{id:"sv-1-458",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A billing team says its new tariff check is a “**statistical solution**” in the book’s sense. Which evidence would justify that label?",options:["A hypothesis test showing the error rate fell, reported with a stated confidence level","A unanimous vote of the most experienced supervisors that the change works","A letter from a large business customer praising the new invoices","An updated process map showing where the new check sits"],answer:0,hint:"Statistical solution = data-driven, with known confidence or risk — not an “I think” solution.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary (p. 589) defines a statistical solution as “a data driven solution with known confidence/risk levels, as opposed to a qualitative, ‘I think’ solution”."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Only the hypothesis test with a stated confidence level gives data plus a known risk."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A vote and a letter are qualitative opinions. A process map shows where the check sits, not whether it works."}],final:"Data with known confidence/risk = statistical solution → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a vote of experts is a qualitative “I think” judgement, the opposite of a statistical solution.","Incorrect — one customer letter is anecdotal evidence with no known confidence or risk level.","Incorrect — a process map documents the change but gives no data on whether it reduced errors."],bookRef:"Book p. 589"},{id:"sv-1-459",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"A contact-centre manager says: “Our **average** wait is 40 seconds, well inside the 60-second target, so customers must be happy.” Yet complaints about long waits keep rising. Which Six Sigma idea BEST explains this?",options:["Customers feel the average, so the complaints must come from a measurement error","The target should be widened, because the process mean already meets it","Customers feel their own result, so wide variation creates defects even with a good average","The process is already at Six Sigma, because its mean is inside the target"],answer:2,hint:"Customers do not experience your average; they experience their own wait.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Defects come mostly from **variation**. A good mean can hide many individual results beyond the limit."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A 40-second average with a wide spread still leaves many callers above 60 seconds — those are the complaints."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Widening the target, blaming measurement or claiming Six Sigma from the mean all ignore the spread."}],final:"Variation around a good average still creates defects → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — customers feel their own result, not the average; there is no evidence of a measurement problem.","Incorrect — widening the target hides the problem; customers rarely accept looser limits.","","Incorrect — the sigma level depends on the spread as well as the mean, so a good average proves nothing."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.1"},{id:"sv-1-460",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"A provisioning process is off-centre and not capable against the customer’s 5-day limit. As the **first** action, the Green Belt asks the customer to change the limit to 7 days. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because widening the limits is the primary objective of Six Sigma projects","Yes, because moving the limits removes the need to reduce variation","No, because customers usually do not accept wider limits, so the process itself must be improved","No, because specification limits may only be changed during the Control phase"],answer:2,hint:"Book options: shift the mean, reduce variation, both (primary objective), or move the limits — which customers usually reject.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (pp. 209–210) lists moving the specification limits as an option that customers “usually do not go for”. The primary objective is to shift the mean **and** reduce variation."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Starting with a request to loosen the customer’s requirement avoids improving the process and is unlikely to succeed."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Wider limits are not the primary objective, and they do not remove variation. No rule ties limit changes to the Control phase."}],final:"Not appropriate — improve the process; customers rarely accept wider limits → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the primary objective is shifting the mean and reducing variation, not widening the limits.","Incorrect — moving the limits leaves the variation unchanged; the process still varies as before.","","Incorrect — there is no rule that limits change only in Control; the issue is customer acceptance."],bookRef:"Book pp. 209–210"},{id:"sv-1-461",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`Three regional repair processes are compared against the same SLA:

\`\`\`
Capability summary — Repair time (h)
Region   StDev   Z.Bench
North     2.1     4.6
South     1.4     2.9
East      3.0     3.8
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is correct?`,options:["South performs best, because it has the smallest standard deviation","North performs best, because its sigma score is the highest","East performs best, because a large standard deviation gives a high sigma level","All three are Six Sigma processes, because every Z.Bench is above 2"],answer:1,hint:"Rank by the sigma score, not by σ alone — Z also depends on where the mean sits.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The sigma score combines the spread **and** the position of the mean relative to the limit. A small σ with a mean close to the limit can still give a low Z."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"North has the highest Z.Bench (4.6), so it has the most standard deviations between its mean and the limit and the fewest expected defects."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"South’s small σ is offset by a mean close to the limit. A larger σ never raises Z. Six Sigma performance needs a sigma level near 6, not just above 2."}],final:"Highest Z.Bench = North → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a small σ alone does not decide it; South’s mean must sit close to the limit, giving the lowest Z.","","Incorrect — a larger σ lowers the sigma level; East ranks second only because of its mean position.","Incorrect — Six Sigma means a sigma level of about 6; values between 2.9 and 4.6 are well below it."],bookRef:"Book p. 588"},{id:"sv-1-462",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A team compares repair time before and after its change:

\`\`\`
Descriptive Statistics: RepairTime (h)
Period   N    Mean    StDev
Before   60   21.8    4.92
After    60   21.6    2.31
USL = 36 h (unchanged)
\`\`\`

Which of the book’s problem-solving options did the team mainly achieve?`,options:["Shift the mean","Shift the mean and reduce variation","Move the specification limits","Reduce variation"],answer:3,hint:"Compare the two rows: which statistic changed a lot, and which hardly moved?",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"The mean barely moved (21.8 → 21.6 h) while the standard deviation roughly halved (4.92 → 2.31 h). The USL did not change."},{title:"Apply the options",body:"A big drop in StDev with a stable mean is the **reduce variation** option (book p. 209)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"No meaningful mean shift occurred, and the USL is unchanged, so the other options do not fit."}],final:"StDev fell sharply, mean unchanged → reduce variation → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the mean moved by only 0.2 h; the real change is in the standard deviation.","Incorrect — doing both would need a clear mean shift as well; here the mean is almost unchanged.","Incorrect — the output states the USL is unchanged, so the limits were not moved.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 209–210"},{id:"sv-1-463",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:3,stem:"According to the book, attribute (pass/fail) data is always treated as **long-term**, in the shifted condition, because so many samples are needed to get a good estimate. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Think about how many pass/fail results are needed before a defect rate becomes reliable.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 223) says “attribute data is always long-term in the shifted condition since it requires so many samples to get a good estimate with reasonable confidence”."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The statement repeats the book’s rule and its reason, so it is true."}],final:"Attribute data = long-term (shifted) → True → answer **True**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the book states exactly this: attribute data needs so many samples that it is always long-term."],bookRef:"Book p. 223"},{id:"sv-1-464",phase:1,topic:"1.1.1",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:1,stem:`Put the book’s four Six Sigma problem-solving boxes in order:

- **P** Correct the statistical problem
- **Q** Define a practical problem
- **R** Apply the correction to the practical problem
- **S** Create a statistical problem`,options:["S → Q → P → R","Q → S → P → R","Q → P → S → R","Q → S → R → P"],answer:1,hint:"Practical → statistical → statistical solution → practical solution.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 208): “Six Sigma always starts with a practical problem, translates it into a statistical problem, corrects the statistical problem and then validates the practical problem.”"},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Define the practical problem (Q), create the statistical problem (S), correct it (P), then apply the correction in practice (R)."}],final:"Q → S → P → R → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a statistical problem cannot be created before the practical problem is defined.","","Incorrect — the problem must be translated into statistics before a statistical correction is possible.","Incorrect — the correction is applied to the practical problem only after the statistical problem is solved."],bookRef:"Book pp. 208, 557"},{id:"sv-1-465",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Motorola engineer Bill Smith argued for processes with a large **safety margin** inside the specification. About how many standard deviations did that margin contain, giving Six Sigma its name?",options:["Three","Four and a half","Six","Twelve"],answer:2,hint:"The name of the method is the size of the margin.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Smith proposed running processes so that about **six standard deviations** fit between the process centre and the specification limit — hence “Six Sigma”."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Three sigma is the traditional control-limit width. Four and a half sigma is the long-term figure left after the 1.5σ shift. Twelve is the full width of a six-sigma process between both limits."}],final:"Six standard deviations inside the specification → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — three sigma is the classic Shewhart control-limit distance, not Smith’s design margin.","Incorrect — 4.5σ is what remains long-term after the conventional 1.5σ shift, not the design margin.","","Incorrect — twelve sigma is the total width between both limits of a centred six-sigma process."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-466",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which quality pioneer is best known for the **cause-and-effect (fishbone) diagram** and for championing **quality circles**?",options:["Armand Feigenbaum","Kaoru Ishikawa","Shigeo Shingo","W. Edwards Deming"],answer:1,hint:"The fishbone diagram is also called the “Ishikawa diagram”.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Kaoru Ishikawa** made quality tools usable by front-line workers in Japan. He is known for the cause-and-effect (fishbone) diagram and for quality circles."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Feigenbaum gave us Total Quality Control. Shingo is linked to SMED and poka-yoke. Deming taught statistical quality control in Japan and wrote the 14 Points."}],final:"Fishbone diagram + quality circles = Ishikawa → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Feigenbaum is known for Total Quality Control (quality is everyone’s job), not the fishbone diagram.","","Incorrect — Shingo is linked to quick changeover (SMED) and mistake-proofing (poka-yoke).","Incorrect — Deming is known for the 14 Points and PDSA, not the fishbone diagram."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-467",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Philip Crosby urged managers to measure the “**price of nonconformance**”. Which Lean Six Sigma concept is a modern descendant of that idea?",options:["Cost of poor quality (COPQ)","Process capability (Cpk)","Rolled throughput yield (RTY)","Takt time"],answer:0,hint:"Crosby wanted the cost of doing things wrong to be visible in money.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Crosby (*Quality Is Free*, 1979) measured the price of nonconformance — the money lost by not doing things right the first time. Today this is the **cost of poor quality (COPQ)**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Cpk and RTY measure performance, not cost. Takt time is the pace of customer demand."}],final:"Price of nonconformance → COPQ → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — Cpk measures how well a process fits its limits; it is not a cost measure.","Incorrect — RTY is the probability of passing all steps first time; it is a yield, not a cost.","Incorrect — takt time is the rate of customer demand, a Lean flow measure, not a quality cost."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-468",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"W. Edwards Deming summarised his management philosophy in a well-known list. What is it called?",options:["The quality trilogy","The seven wastes","The DMAIC roadmap","The 14 Points for Management"],answer:3,hint:"Deming wrote a numbered list of obligations for management.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Deming set out his philosophy as the **14 Points for Management** (in *Out of the Crisis*, 1980s), stressing that most quality problems belong to the system, which management owns."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The trilogy is Juran’s. The seven wastes come from the Toyota Production System. DMAIC is the Six Sigma roadmap from Motorola."}],final:"Deming = 14 Points for Management → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the quality trilogy (planning, control, improvement) is Joseph Juran’s framework.","Incorrect — the seven wastes come from Toyota’s production system (Ohno), the root of Lean.","Incorrect — DMAIC is the Six Sigma improvement roadmap, credited to Motorola.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-469",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:3,stem:"The idea of the “vital few and trivial many” was first applied to **quality problems** by the economist Vilfredo Pareto himself. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Separate who made the original observation from who applied it to quality.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Vilfredo Pareto (1848–1923) studied the distribution of **wealth**. **Joseph Juran** later applied the idea to quality and named it the Pareto principle."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The statement credits Pareto with the quality application, so it is false."}],final:"Juran, not Pareto, applied it to quality → False → answer **False**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Pareto studied wealth; it was Joseph Juran who applied the idea to quality and named it.",""],trap:"Pareto = wealth; Juran = quality.",bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-470",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What business situation pushed **Motorola** to create Six Sigma in the 1980s?",options:["It had just won the Deming Prize and wanted to defend its title","United States law began to require defect rates below 3.4 DPMO","It was losing ground to Japanese competitors on quality","General Electric demanded defect-free parts from its suppliers"],answer:2,hint:"Think about the US–Japan quality gap of the early 1980s.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"By the early 1980s Motorola was **losing ground to Japanese competitors on quality**. Bill Smith’s analysis and Bob Galvin’s backing led to Six Sigma."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The Deming Prize is a Japanese award Motorola did not hold. No law sets 3.4 DPMO. GE adopted Six Sigma only in 1995, after Motorola."}],final:"Losing to Japanese quality → Six Sigma → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the Deming Prize is awarded in Japan by JUSE; it did not drive Motorola’s programme.","Incorrect — 3.4 DPMO is a voluntary stretch target, not a legal requirement.","","Incorrect — GE adopted Six Sigma in 1995, years after Motorola created it."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-471",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which idea is most closely linked with the quality movement led by **Feigenbaum and Ishikawa** from the 1950s to the 1970s?",options:["Statistical control charts that separate common-cause from special-cause variation","Quality as everyone’s job, supported by simple tools used by front-line workers","Defect rates measured per million opportunities with a 1.5σ allowance","Waste removed through just-in-time flow and pull from the customer"],answer:1,hint:"Match each era to its main idea: 1920s statistics, 1950s–70s everyone’s job, 1980s DPMO, Toyota flow.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Feigenbaum’s **Total Quality Control** said quality cannot be inspected in by one department; Ishikawa gave workers simple tools and quality circles. Together: **quality is everyone’s job**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Control charts are Shewhart’s 1920s contribution. DPMO with a 1.5σ shift is Motorola’s Six Sigma. Just-in-time flow is the Toyota strand."}],final:"Feigenbaum + Ishikawa = quality is everyone’s job → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — control charts and common versus special cause are Walter Shewhart’s 1920s ideas.","","Incorrect — defects per million opportunities with a 1.5σ shift is Motorola’s Six Sigma metric.","Incorrect — just-in-time and pull belong to the Toyota Production System, the root of Lean."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-472",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which Toyota engineer, working from the late 1940s to the 1970s, turned the Toyoda family’s ideas into the **Toyota Production System**?",options:["Kaoru Ishikawa","Shigeo Shingo","Taiichi Ohno","Walter Shewhart"],answer:2,hint:"TPS architect, author of the book *Toyota Production System*.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Taiichi Ohno** built the Toyota Production System, using tools such as kanban and a constant attack on waste (muda). It became known in the West as Lean."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Shingo was an outside consultant who contributed SMED and poka-yoke. Ishikawa is the fishbone pioneer. Shewhart created the control chart at the Bell telephone system."}],final:"TPS architect = Taiichi Ohno → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Ishikawa is known for the fishbone diagram and quality circles, not for building TPS.","Incorrect — Shingo contributed SMED and poka-yoke as a consultant, but Ohno built the system.","","Incorrect — Shewhart worked on statistical control charts in the US, not at Toyota."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-473",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Walter Shewhart’s key insight was that all processes vary and that variation comes in two kinds. Which two?",options:["Common-cause and special-cause variation","Short-term and long-term variation","Repeatability and reproducibility","Within-piece and piece-to-piece variation"],answer:0,hint:"One kind is built into the system; the other is an unusual signal.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Shewhart separated ordinary, built-in **common-cause** variation from unusual **special-cause** signals. Reacting to every common-cause wiggle makes things worse."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Short-term/long-term is a capability idea. Repeatability/reproducibility are Gage R&R components. Within-piece/piece-to-piece are multi-vari families."}],final:"Shewhart: common cause vs special cause → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — short-term versus long-term data is a capability idea linked to the 1.5σ shift.","Incorrect — repeatability and reproducibility are the two parts of measurement-system (Gage R&R) error.","Incorrect — within-piece and piece-to-piece are families of variation studied with a multi-vari chart."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-474",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What was the central argument of Philip Crosby’s 1979 book ***Quality Is Free***?",options:["Quality improvement must be paid for by raising prices to customers","A large final inspection team is the cheapest way to guarantee quality","Variation can be ignored as long as the average meets the target","Doing things right the first time costs less than fixing failures"],answer:3,hint:"The title means that the money saved on failures pays for the quality effort.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Crosby argued that **doing things right the first time costs less than fixing failures**, so quality effort pays for itself."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Raising prices and big inspection teams add cost rather than prevent failures. Ignoring variation contradicts the whole quality movement."}],final:"Right first time is cheaper than fixing failures → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Crosby’s point is that quality pays for itself through avoided failures, not through higher prices.","Incorrect — inspection finds failures after they happen and adds cost; Crosby stressed prevention.","Incorrect — ignoring variation is not Crosby’s idea; customers feel each result, not the average.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-475",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which statement about the Toyota Production System and Six Sigma is CORRECT?",options:["DMAIC belongs to Six Sigma, not to the Toyota Production System","Kanban was created at Motorola as part of the first Six Sigma programme","The Toyota Production System was developed after GE adopted Six Sigma","Six Sigma began as Toyota’s method for reducing waiting time"],answer:0,hint:"Toyota → Lean (waste, flow, kanban). Motorola → Six Sigma (DMAIC, variation).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"DMAIC is the Six Sigma roadmap credited to **Motorola**. The Toyota Production System gave us kanban, just-in-time and waste elimination."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Kanban is a Toyota tool. TPS was built from the 1940s to the 1970s, long before GE’s 1995 launch. Six Sigma was born at Motorola, not Toyota."}],final:"DMAIC = Six Sigma, not TPS → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — kanban is a Toyota Production System tool, not a Motorola Six Sigma invention.","Incorrect — TPS was developed between the 1940s and 1970s, well before GE adopted Six Sigma in 1995.","Incorrect — Six Sigma began at Motorola as a defect-reduction method, not at Toyota."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-476",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about **Larry Bossidy** is CORRECT?",options:["He invented Six Sigma while working as an engineer at Motorola","He coined the term “Black Belt” while training staff at Unisys","He was a senior GE executive before leading AlliedSignal’s Six Sigma programme","He led Motorola when it won the first Baldrige award in 1988"],answer:2,hint:"Bossidy: GE vice-chairman in the 1980s, AlliedSignal CEO from 1991.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Bossidy was **vice-chairman of GE** (1984–91), then became CEO of **AlliedSignal** in 1991 and made Six Sigma central there in the early 1990s. In 1995 he spoke to GE’s senior executives about it."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The Motorola engineer was Bill Smith. Mikel Harry coined “Black Belt”. Bob Galvin led Motorola at the 1988 Baldrige award."}],final:"Bossidy = ex-GE executive who led AlliedSignal’s Six Sigma → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the Motorola engineer credited with Six Sigma is Bill Smith.","Incorrect — the term “Black Belt” is credited to Mikel Harry, from his work at Unisys.","","Incorrect — Bob Galvin led Motorola when it received the first Baldrige award in 1988."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-477",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the spread of Six Sigma **beyond manufacturing** is CORRECT?",options:["Six Sigma was designed for banking and only later moved into factories","GE’s publicised savings helped carry Six Sigma into services, finance and telecoms","Six Sigma cannot be used in service processes because they have no defects","Six Sigma reached services only after Lean had replaced DMAIC"],answer:1,hint:"GE turned Six Sigma into a global management movement in the late 1990s.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"GE’s public reports of large savings did most to make Six Sigma a global movement and to spread it into **services, finance and telecoms**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Six Sigma began in electronics manufacturing at Motorola. Service processes have defects too (wrong bills, missed SLAs). DMAIC was never replaced; Lean tools were added inside it."}],final:"GE’s savings carried Six Sigma into services → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Six Sigma began in Motorola’s electronics manufacturing and moved into services later.","","Incorrect — service processes have defects too, such as billing errors and missed SLAs.","Incorrect — DMAIC was not replaced; Lean Six Sigma uses DMAIC as its backbone with Lean tools inside."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-478",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:1,stem:`Which statements about quality pioneers are CORRECT?

1. Philip Crosby invented the fishbone diagram.
2. Armand Feigenbaum coined the term “lean production”.
3. Walter Shewhart drew the first control chart.
4. Joseph Juran framed quality planning, control and improvement as a trilogy.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:2,hint:"Match each pioneer to the contribution they are actually known for.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 is false — the fishbone is Ishikawa’s. 2 is false — “lean production” was coined by John Krafcik (1988). 3 is true — Shewhart, 1924. 4 is true — the Juran Trilogy."},{title:"Match a pair",body:"The true statements are 3 and 4."}],final:"True statements are 3 and 4 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — both are false: the fishbone is Ishikawa’s and “lean” was coined by Krafcik.","Incorrect — includes statement 2; Feigenbaum is known for Total Quality Control, not the word “lean”.","","Incorrect — includes statement 1; Crosby is known for zero defects, not the fishbone diagram."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-479",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which two statements about **Motorola’s** role in Six Sigma are CORRECT?

1. Six Sigma originated at Motorola in the mid-1980s.
2. Motorola received the Deming Prize in 1951 for its Six Sigma programme.
3. Motorola adopted Six Sigma after learning it from General Electric.
4. Motorola’s chief executive, Bob Galvin, backed the programme.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:3,hint:"Motorola originated it (mid-1980s, Galvin backing); GE came later; the Deming Prize is Japanese.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 is true. 2 is false — the Deming Prize began in 1951, decades before Six Sigma, and is a Japanese award; Motorola won the US Baldrige award in 1988. 3 is false — GE learned from Motorola, not the reverse. 4 is true."},{title:"Match a pair",body:"The true statements are 1 and 4."}],final:"True statements are 1 and 4 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — includes statement 2; Six Sigma did not exist in 1951, and Motorola’s 1988 award was the Baldrige.","Incorrect — both are false: no 1951 Deming Prize for Six Sigma, and GE followed Motorola.","Incorrect — includes statement 3; GE adopted Six Sigma in 1995, after Motorola originated it.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-480",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

[?] argued that quality cannot be inspected in by one department and is everyone’s job, an idea he called Total Quality Control.`,options:["Crosby","Feigenbaum","Ishikawa","Juran"],answer:1,hint:"Total Quality Control → the same pioneer whose idea fed into TQM.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Armand Feigenbaum** (1950s) argued quality is everyone’s job, from design to service, and called it **Total Quality Control**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Crosby is linked to zero defects; Ishikawa to the fishbone and quality circles; Juran to the trilogy and the Pareto principle."}],final:"Total Quality Control = Feigenbaum → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Crosby is known for zero defects and Quality Is Free, not Total Quality Control.","","Incorrect — Ishikawa is known for the fishbone diagram and quality circles.","Incorrect — Juran is known for the quality trilogy and for naming the Pareto principle."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-481",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

When Lean and Six Sigma merged in the early 2000s, organisations usually kept [?] as the backbone, with Lean tools used inside it.`,options:["DMAIC","kanban","PDSA","TPM"],answer:0,hint:"The combined approach kept the five-phase Six Sigma roadmap.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Lean Six Sigma typically uses **DMAIC** (Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve, Control) as its backbone, with Lean tools such as value stream maps, 5S and kanban inside the phases."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Kanban and TPM are Lean tools used inside DMAIC. PDSA is Deming’s learning cycle, not the Lean Six Sigma roadmap."}],final:"Backbone of Lean Six Sigma = DMAIC → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — kanban is a Lean pull tool that can be used inside DMAIC, not the backbone.","Incorrect — PDSA is Deming’s improvement cycle; Lean Six Sigma is built on DMAIC.","Incorrect — Total Productive Maintenance is a Lean tool, not the Lean Six Sigma roadmap."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-482",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT a contribution of **Kaoru Ishikawa**?",options:["The cause-and-effect (fishbone) diagram","Quality circles of front-line employees","Making simple quality tools usable by workers","The first statistical control chart"],answer:3,hint:"Separate the Japanese worker-tool pioneer from the 1920s statistician.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Ishikawa gave us the fishbone diagram, quality circles and the push to make quality tools usable on the front line."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The first control chart was drawn by **Walter Shewhart** in 1924, not by Ishikawa."}],final:"First control chart = Shewhart, not Ishikawa → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the fishbone diagram is Ishikawa’s best-known contribution.","Incorrect — Ishikawa championed quality circles from the early 1960s.","Incorrect — making tools accessible to front-line workers was central to Ishikawa’s work.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-483",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"Lean Six Sigma grew from several strands of continuous improvement. Which is NOT one of them?",options:["Statistical quality control, from Shewhart onward","Crosby’s zero-defects standard as the Six Sigma target","The Toyota Production System","Motorola’s Six Sigma programme"],answer:1,hint:"Three strands braided together: statistical control, Toyota (Lean) and Motorola (Six Sigma).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The history has three strands: **statistical quality control** (1920s onward), the **Toyota Production System** (Lean) and **Six Sigma** from Motorola."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Crosby’s zero-defects slogan was influential, but Six Sigma did not adopt it as its target; it uses 3.4 DPMO, a small non-zero rate."}],final:"Zero defects is not Six Sigma’s target → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — statistical quality control is the first strand of Lean Six Sigma.","","Incorrect — the Toyota Production System is the strand that became Lean.","Incorrect — Motorola’s Six Sigma is the strand that gives DMAIC and the 3.4 DPMO metric."],trap:"Six Sigma = 3.4 DPMO, not zero defects.",bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-484",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A billing manager blames errors on “careless staff” and introduces fines for each mistake, without changing systems, training or procedures. Which pioneer’s teaching most directly contradicts this approach?",options:["Bill Smith, who argued for a six-sigma safety margin","Philip Crosby, who measured the price of nonconformance","W. Edwards Deming, who taught that most problems belong to the system","Taiichi Ohno, who built the Toyota Production System"],answer:2,hint:"Who said most quality problems come from the system, which only management can change?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"**Deming** taught that most quality problems belong to the **system**, which only management can change. Blaming and fining individuals ignores the system."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The manager changes nothing in the system and punishes people — exactly what Deming warned against."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Smith’s margin, Crosby’s cost measure and Ohno’s waste focus are real contributions, but none is the “system, not the worker” teaching."}],final:"System, not worker → Deming → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Smith’s idea concerns design margin against variation, not management blaming staff.","Incorrect — Crosby measured the cost of failures; the “problems belong to the system” teaching is Deming’s.","","Incorrect — Ohno focused on waste and flow in production, not on this management-blame point."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-485",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A repair ticket waits six hours in a queue for a dispatcher, and one repair in twenty fails the first time. Why does a **Lean Six Sigma** approach suit this process?",options:["Lean tackles the queue (waste and flow), while Six Sigma tackles the repeat failures (variation and defects)","Lean tackles the repeat failures, while Six Sigma tackles the queue","Only Lean is needed, because both problems are forms of waiting","Only Six Sigma is needed, because DMAIC cannot use Lean tools"],answer:0,hint:"Lean = speed and waste. Six Sigma = consistency and defects.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Lean and Six Sigma solve two halves of one problem: **Lean removes waste and speeds up flow; Six Sigma removes variation and defects**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The six-hour wait is a flow and waste problem (Lean). The one-in-twenty failure is a defect and variation problem (Six Sigma)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Reversing the roles, or choosing only one approach, misses half of the process’s problems."}],final:"Queue → Lean; repeat failures → Six Sigma → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — this reverses the roles: waiting time is Lean’s focus and defects are Six Sigma’s.","Incorrect — a failed repair is a defect, not waiting; it needs Six Sigma’s variation tools.","Incorrect — Lean Six Sigma deliberately uses Lean tools inside the DMAIC phases."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-486",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"A Green Belt’s history slide places the combined “Lean Six Sigma” approach in the **1970s**, before Motorola’s Six Sigma. A reviewer moves it to the **early 2000s**. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because Lean and Six Sigma were combined only after both traditions had matured, in the early 2000s","Yes, because Lean Six Sigma was created at GE in 1995 as a replacement for Six Sigma","No, because Lean Six Sigma was the original Toyota method that Motorola later simplified","No, because Lean Six Sigma began in the 1920s with Shewhart’s control charts"],answer:0,hint:"Six Sigma (mid-1980s) and Lean (named 1988) had to exist before they could merge.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Six Sigma was created at Motorola in the mid-1980s, and Toyota’s system was named “lean” in 1988. Organisations began **combining them in the early 2000s**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A 1970s date is impossible, because Six Sigma did not yet exist. The reviewer’s correction is right."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"GE in 1995 deployed Six Sigma, not Lean Six Sigma. Toyota and Shewhart are earlier roots, not the merged approach."}],final:"Appropriate — the merger came in the early 2000s → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — GE deployed Six Sigma itself from 1995; the Lean merger came later and did not replace it.","Incorrect — Toyota’s system became Lean; it was not called Lean Six Sigma and Motorola did not simplify it.","Incorrect — Shewhart’s 1920s work is a root of quality control, not the merged Lean Six Sigma approach."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-487",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"Armand Feigenbaum’s idea of Total Quality Control fed into what later became Total Quality Management (TQM). True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Total Quality *Control* → Total Quality *Management*.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Feigenbaum’s **Total Quality Control** (1950s) said quality is everyone’s job. The idea fed into **TQM** and the modern view of quality costs."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The statement matches the history, so it is true."}],final:"TQC fed into TQM → True → answer **True**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — Feigenbaum’s Total Quality Control is a recognised forerunner of Total Quality Management."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-488",phase:1,topic:"1.1.2",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:3,stem:`Put these **main ideas** of continuous improvement in the order in which they became prominent:

- **P** Defects per million opportunities and the DMAIC roadmap
- **Q** Statistical control of variation with control charts
- **R** Combining waste reduction and variation reduction in one approach
- **S** Quality as everyone’s job, with simple tools for front-line workers`,options:["Q → P → S → R","S → Q → P → R","Q → S → R → P","Q → S → P → R"],answer:3,hint:"1920s statistics → 1950s–70s everyone’s job → mid-1980s Six Sigma → early 2000s Lean Six Sigma.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Shewhart’s control charts (1920s) came first (Q). Feigenbaum and Ishikawa spread “quality is everyone’s job” (1950s–70s, S). Motorola created DPMO and DMAIC (mid-1980s, P). Lean Six Sigma combined both streams (early 2000s, R)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The order is Q → S → P → R."}],final:"Q → S → P → R → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — puts Six Sigma (1980s) before the 1950s–70s “everyone’s job” movement.","Incorrect — the 1950s–70s movement came after Shewhart’s 1920s control charts.","Incorrect — Lean Six Sigma combined the streams only after Six Sigma existed, so R cannot precede P.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.2"},{id:"sv-1-489",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Each DMAIC phase answers one question. Which phase answers: “**How big is the problem today, and can we trust our data?**”",options:["Analyze","Control","Define","Measure"],answer:3,hint:"Baseline size + trustworthy measurement system = the phase after Define.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Define frames the problem; **Measure** sizes it (baseline, capability) and checks the data can be trusted (MSA); Analyze finds verified causes; Improve fixes them; Control sustains the gain."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"“How big today” = baseline; “trust our data” = measurement system analysis. Both are Measure deliverables."}],final:"Baseline and trustworthy data = Measure → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Analyze answers “what are the verified root causes?”, after the baseline exists.","Incorrect — Control answers “how do we keep the gain and prove the benefit?”.","Incorrect — Define answers “what is the problem, why does it matter and what is success?”.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 230"},{id:"sv-1-490",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"On the book’s DMAIC roadmap, the first box — “**Identify Problem Area**” — sits in whose lane, before the Define steps begin?",options:["The Green Belt and the project team","The Master Black Belt and Finance","The Champion and the Process Owner","The customer and the supplier"],answer:2,hint:"Problem areas are chosen by the people who own the business results, before a Belt is assigned.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s roadmap (p. 230) places “Identify Problem Area” in the **Champion / Process Owner** lane. The Define steps that follow are determine project focus, estimate COPQ and establish the team."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The Belt and team start work at Define. The MBB coaches and Finance validates savings. Customers give VOC but do not own the roadmap step."}],final:"Identify problem area = Champion / Process Owner → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the Belt and team take over at the Define steps, after the problem area is identified.","Incorrect — the MBB coaches Belts and Finance validates benefits; neither owns this first step.","","Incorrect — customers supply VOC, but they are not a lane on the book’s DMAIC roadmap."],bookRef:"Book p. 230"},{id:"sv-1-491",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"What does the book name as the **last physical result** of the Control phase?",options:["The capability report","The control plan","The FMEA","The project charter"],answer:1,hint:"The document the process owner keeps using after the Belt leaves.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 556) states: “The last physical result of the Control Phase is the **Control Plan**.”"},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The charter is a Define output. Capability and FMEA are produced in Measure (and updated later) but are not the final Control result."}],final:"Last physical result of Control = control plan → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — capability is first assessed in Measure; it may be re-checked but is not the final Control output.","","Incorrect — the FMEA is built in Measure and updated later; it is not the last physical result.","Incorrect — the charter is the central Define deliverable, produced at the start."],bookRef:"Book p. 556"},{id:"sv-1-492",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"The book’s deliverables lists for the Measure, Analyze and Improve phases share a **common core**. Which set is it?",options:["MSA, stability graphs, capability analysis, FMEA and X-Y matrix","Team members, primary metric, secondary metrics, project plan, issues and barriers","Data demographics, hypothesis testing, modelling and strategy to reduce the X’s","Experiment justification, experiment plan and experiment results"],answer:1,hint:"Look for the items that appear whatever the phase.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Each phase list (pp. 229, 416, 489) repeats **team members, primary metric, secondary metric(s), project plan, and issues and barriers**, then adds phase-specific items."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"MSA, stability, capability, FMEA and X-Y matrix are Measure-specific. Hypothesis testing and modelling are Analyze-specific. Experiment items are Improve-specific."}],final:"Common core = team, primary/secondary metrics, plan, issues and barriers → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — these are Measure-specific deliverables, not the core shared by every phase.","","Incorrect — these are Analyze-specific deliverables listed on top of the common core.","Incorrect — these are Improve-specific deliverables listed on top of the common core."],bookRef:"Book pp. 229, 416, 489"},{id:"sv-1-493",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"According to the book, to whom does the Belt present each phase’s deliverables?",options:["The mentor and the project Champion","The customer and the supplier","The finance team and the auditors","The team members only"],answer:0,hint:"The tollgate audience is the coach and the sponsor.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 229) says the phase deliverables are presented “to their **mentor and project Champion**”."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Customers, auditors and team members may see results, but the review audience named is the mentor and Champion."}],final:"Deliverables go to the mentor and the Champion → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — customers and suppliers give inputs, but they are not the tollgate audience named in the book.","Incorrect — Finance validates savings, but the book names the mentor and Champion as the review audience.","Incorrect — the team helps prepare the deliverables; the review itself is with the mentor and Champion."],bookRef:"Book p. 229"},{id:"sv-1-494",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"What does the book state as the **purpose of the Control phase**?",options:["To find and verify the root causes of the problem","To validate the measurement system before collecting data","To agree the project scope and goals with the sponsor","To ensure that the gains made by the project remain in place"],answer:3,hint:"Control = sustain.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 498) describes the Control phase as an attempt to ensure that **the gains made with the project remain in place**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Verifying root causes is Analyze. Validating the measurement system is Measure. Agreeing scope and goals is Define."}],final:"Control = keep the gains in place → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — finding and verifying root causes is the purpose of Analyze.","Incorrect — validating the measurement system is a Measure deliverable.","Incorrect — agreeing scope and goals happens in Define through the charter.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 498"},{id:"sv-1-495",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"Which description matches the book’s **DMAIC Phases Roadmap**?",options:["A flow chart of the goals to be reached during each DMAIC phase","A Gantt chart of the project’s tasks and dates","A high-level map of suppliers, inputs, process, outputs and customers","A matrix ranking potential inputs against the project outputs"],answer:0,hint:"Roadmap = goals per phase, drawn as a flow chart.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 230) says: “The DMAIC Phases Roadmap is a **flow chart of what goals should be reached during each phase** of DMAIC.”"},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A Gantt chart is a project plan. A supplier-to-customer map is a SIPOC. A matrix ranking inputs against outputs is the X-Y matrix."}],final:"DMAIC roadmap = flow chart of goals per phase → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a Gantt chart schedules tasks; it is the project plan, not the DMAIC roadmap.","Incorrect — a map of suppliers, inputs, process, outputs and customers is a SIPOC.","Incorrect — a matrix ranking inputs against outputs is the X-Y matrix."],bookRef:"Book p. 230"},{id:"sv-1-496",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which item appears in the book’s list of **Analyze-phase** deliverables?",options:["Experiment plan and objective","MSA and stability graphs","Hypothesis testing and modelling results","Documentation and response plans"],answer:2,hint:"Analyze proves or disproves the X’s with data.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s Analyze list (p. 416) adds **data demographics, hypothesis testing, modelling and a strategy to reduce the X’s** to the common core."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Experiment plans are Improve deliverables. MSA and stability graphs are Measure deliverables. Documentation and response plans belong to Control."}],final:"Hypothesis testing and modelling = Analyze → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — experiment justification, plan and results are Improve-phase deliverables.","Incorrect — MSA and stability graphs are Measure-phase deliverables.","","Incorrect — documentation and response plans are part of the Control phase."],bookRef:"Book p. 416"},{id:"sv-1-497",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"How does the book state the **goal of the Improve phase**?",options:["To locate the variables that significantly affect the primary metric","To determine the optimal levels of the variables that significantly affect the primary metric","To quantify the baseline capability of the primary metric","To ensure the gains in the primary metric remain in place"],answer:1,hint:"Analyze *locates* the important variables; Improve finds their *best settings*.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 489): the goal of Improve is to “determine the **optimal levels** of the variables which are significantly impacting your Primary Metric”."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Locating the variables is the Analyze goal (p. 415). Baseline capability is Measure. Keeping the gains is Control."}],final:"Improve = optimal levels of the significant variables → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — locating the significant variables is the goal of Analyze, the phase before Improve.","","Incorrect — quantifying baseline capability is a Measure deliverable.","Incorrect — keeping the gains in place is the purpose of the Control phase."],trap:"Locate the X’s = Analyze; set the X’s at optimal levels = Improve.",bookRef:"Book pp. 415, 489"},{id:"sv-1-498",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"At a tollgate review, which decisions can the Champion make?",options:["Approve, escalate or delegate the project to another Belt","Plan, do or check the next phase","Accept, reject or defer the baseline data","Go to the next phase, recycle to fix gaps, or stop the project"],answer:3,hint:"A tollgate is a decision gate based on evidence.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"At a tollgate the Champion reviews the phase deliverables and decides **go** (proceed), **recycle** (fix gaps and return) or **stop** (the project is no longer worth it)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The other lists mix management verbs, the PDCA cycle and data-acceptance terms; none is the tollgate decision set."}],final:"Tollgate decisions = go, recycle or stop → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — escalating or delegating are general management actions, not the tollgate decision set.","Incorrect — plan, do, check are steps of the PDCA cycle, not tollgate outcomes.","Incorrect — judging baseline data is part of Measure work, not the tollgate decision itself.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.3"},{id:"sv-1-499",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"How are a project’s financial benefits handled across the DMAIC phases?",options:["By verifying them in Define and estimating them again in Control","By estimating them in Define and verifying them in Control","By calculating them only once, in Improve, after the pilot","By leaving them to Finance after closure, without the Belt’s involvement"],answer:1,hint:"Business case and COPQ = estimates; “Verify Financial Impact” = the last Control step.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The business case and preliminary COPQ in Define are **estimates**. Real savings are confirmed at the end of Control in the roadmap step “**Verify Financial Impact**”."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Nothing can be verified before the change exists. Pilot results are evidence, not the final financial check. The Control checklist asks Champion, Belt and Finance to agree together."}],final:"Benefits: estimated in Define, verified in Control → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — this reverses the order; benefits cannot be verified before any change has been made.","","Incorrect — pilot results support the solution, but financial impact is verified at the end of Control.","Incorrect — the book asks the Champion, Belt and Finance to agree before the project is complete."],bookRef:"Book pp. 230, 578"},{id:"sv-1-500",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about the book’s **Control-phase checklist** is CORRECT?",options:["It asks whether the customer has signed the project charter","It asks whether a designed experiment was run in every phase","It asks whether the Champion, the Belt and Finance all agree the project is complete","It asks whether the root causes are still to be identified"],answer:2,hint:"Closure needs three parties to agree.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Control checklist (p. 578) includes “Has the Control Plan been handed off to the process owner?” and “**Do the Champion, the Belt and Finance all agree this project is complete?**”."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Charter sign-off belongs to Define. The book says most problems do not need DOE. Root causes should already be verified in Analyze."}],final:"Control checklist: Champion, Belt and Finance agree the project is complete → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the charter is agreed between the team and its sponsor in Define, not signed by the customer at Control.","Incorrect — the book says most problems will not need designed experiments.","","Incorrect — root causes should already be verified in Analyze, well before Control."],bookRef:"Book p. 578"},{id:"sv-1-501",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"Which statement about **designed experiments** in the Improve phase is CORRECT, according to the book?",options:["Most problems will not need designed experiments, so they are used only as necessary","Every Improve phase must include a full factorial experiment","A project cannot pass the Improve tollgate without a designed experiment","Designed experiments are the main deliverable of the Define phase"],answer:0,hint:"The book warns against “analysis paralysis”.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 489) advises: avoid analysis paralysis and “only use DOE’s as necessary. Most problems will NOT require the use of Designed Experiments.”"},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Making DOE compulsory contradicts that advice. Define deliverables are about framing the problem (charter, CTQs), not experiments."}],final:"DOE only as necessary — most problems do not need it → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the book says most problems do not require designed experiments, let alone full factorials.","Incorrect — the Improve tollgate needs evidence the solution works; a DOE is used only when necessary.","Incorrect — Define deliverables frame the problem (charter, CTQs, COPQ estimate), not experiments."],bookRef:"Book p. 489"},{id:"sv-1-502",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about the book’s **Measure-phase roadmap** is CORRECT?",options:["It is a strict rule, and any change of step order invalidates the project","It starts with verifying the financial impact of the project","It ends with the selection of the solution to pilot","It is more of a guideline than a rule"],answer:3,hint:"The book describes its own roadmap with a short caution about how strictly to follow it.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 230) presents the Measure roadmap — detailed problem statement through to “Review Progress with Champion” — and notes it is “**more of a guideline than a rule**”."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Financial impact is verified at the end of Control. Solutions are selected in Improve. The roadmap starts with the detailed problem statement and ends with the Champion review."}],final:"Measure roadmap = guideline, not a strict rule → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book itself says the roadmap is more of a guideline than a rule.","Incorrect — verifying financial impact is the final step of Control, not the start of Measure.","Incorrect — the Measure roadmap ends with a review of progress with the Champion; solutions come in Improve.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 230"},{id:"sv-1-503",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:2,stem:`Which statements about the **Measure-phase deliverables** listed in the book are CORRECT?

1. They include the verified root causes of the problem.
2. They include a measurement system analysis (MSA).
3. They include an X-Y matrix and an FMEA.
4. They include the results of the designed experiment.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:1,hint:"Measure list: process map, FMEA, X-Y matrix, basic statistics on Y, MSA, stability graphs, capability.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 is false — verified root causes are Analyze. 2 is true. 3 is true — the X-Y matrix and FMEA prioritise the X’s in Measure. 4 is false — experiment results are Improve."},{title:"Match a pair",body:"The true statements are 2 and 3."}],final:"True statements are 2 and 3 → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — includes statement 1; root causes are verified in Analyze, not Measure.","","Incorrect — includes statement 4; experiment results are an Improve deliverable.","Incorrect — both are false: root causes belong to Analyze and experiment results to Improve."],bookRef:"Book p. 229"},{id:"sv-1-504",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:3,stem:`The book’s project status review lists what each phase achieves. Which statements describe what is achieved in **Define**?

1. The team has validated the measurement system.
2. The team has proven which X’s affect the output.
3. The team understands the problem and its impact on the business.
4. The team has established firm goals for improvement.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:2,hint:"Define is about framing: problem, impact, goals and the output characteristic — not proof.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 is false — validating the measurement system is Measure. 2 is false — proving X’s needs data (Analyze/Improve). 3 and 4 are true — the review (p. 473) lists understanding the problem and its business impact, and setting firm goals, as Define outcomes."},{title:"Match a pair",body:"The true statements are 3 and 4."}],final:"True statements are 3 and 4 → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — both are false: MSA is Measure work and proving X’s needs data from later phases.","Incorrect — includes statement 2; proving which X’s matter is not a Define outcome.","","Incorrect — includes statement 1; the measurement system is validated in Measure."],bookRef:"Book p. 473"},{id:"sv-1-505",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"statements",difficulty:1,stem:`Which two statements about **when** project work happens are CORRECT?

1. Suspected causes may be listed in Define.
2. Root causes are verified with data in Analyze.
3. The measurement system is first validated in Improve.
4. Pilot results are a Define deliverable.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:0,hint:"List suspicions early; prove them in Analyze; validate measurement in Measure; pilot in Improve.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 is true — Define may note suspicions. 2 is true — proof with data is Analyze work. 3 is false — MSA is a Measure deliverable. 4 is false — piloting is Improve."},{title:"Match a pair",body:"The true statements are 1 and 2."}],final:"True statements are 1 and 2 → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — includes statement 3; the measurement system is validated in Measure, before analysis.","Incorrect — both are false: MSA belongs to Measure and pilots belong to Improve.","Incorrect — includes statement 4; a pilot tests a solution in Improve, long after Define."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.3"},{id:"sv-1-506",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

At the end of each DMAIC phase, a [?] review decides, on the evidence of the deliverables, whether the project may proceed.`,options:["kaizen","milestone","peer","tollgate"],answer:3,hint:"The name comes from a barrier you pass only after paying (showing evidence).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **tollgate** review is the formal checkpoint at the end of each phase, where the Champion decides go, recycle or stop."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A kaizen is a rapid improvement event. A milestone is a date on the plan. A peer review is an informal check by colleagues."}],final:"End-of-phase decision = tollgate review → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a kaizen is a short, focused improvement event, not an end-of-phase decision gate.","Incorrect — a milestone is a planned date; the decision meeting at the end of a phase is the tollgate.","Incorrect — a peer review is an informal check by colleagues with no go/stop authority.",""],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.3"},{id:"sv-1-507",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:1,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

The central Define-phase document, agreed between the team and its sponsor, is the project [?].`,options:["charter","closure","plan","review"],answer:0,hint:"One page holding business case, problem, goal, scope, team and milestones.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **project charter** is the core Define deliverable: business case, problem statement, goal statement, scope, team and milestones."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The project plan is a supporting schedule repeated in every phase. Closure is the end of Control. A review is a tollgate event, not the Define document."}],final:"Central Define document = project charter → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — project closure happens at the end of Control, after the financial impact is verified.","Incorrect — the project plan is a schedule repeated in every phase’s deliverables, not the central Define document.","Incorrect — a review is the tollgate meeting where deliverables are judged, not a deliverable itself."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.3"},{id:"sv-1-508",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT one of the **Improve-phase** deliverables listed in the book?",options:["Experiment justification","Experiment plan and objective","Measurement system analysis","Experiment results"],answer:2,hint:"Improve-specific items all concern the experiment; MSA comes much earlier.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Improve list (p. 489) adds experiment justification, experiment plan/objective and experiment results to the common core."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"MSA is a **Measure** deliverable: the data must be trustworthy before analysis starts."}],final:"MSA is a Measure deliverable, not Improve → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — experiment justification is listed among the Improve deliverables.","Incorrect — the experiment plan and objective are listed among the Improve deliverables.","","Incorrect — experiment results are listed among the Improve deliverables."],bookRef:"Book p. 489"},{id:"sv-1-509",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:3,stem:"Which is NOT a **Control-phase roadblock** named in the book?",options:["Lack of project sign-off","Too many potential X’s still listed on the X-Y matrix","Financial benefits not tracked and integrated into the business","Lack of buy-in from process operators or staff"],answer:1,hint:"Control roadblocks are about sustaining and closing, not about narrowing the X’s.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 577) lists Control roadblocks such as lack of project sign-off, team members not involved in the control plan design, benefits not tracked and integrated, and lack of operator buy-in."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"A long list of potential X’s is a Measure-phase issue, solved with the X-Y matrix and FMEA long before Control."}],final:"Too many potential X’s is a Measure issue, not a Control roadblock → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — lack of project sign-off is a Control roadblock named by the book.","","Incorrect — untracked financial benefits are a Control roadblock named by the book.","Incorrect — lack of buy-in from operators or staff is a Control roadblock named by the book."],bookRef:"Book p. 577"},{id:"sv-1-510",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:2,stem:"Which is NOT one of the **Measure-phase roadblocks** described in the book?",options:["The Champion has approved the project charter","Team members do not have time to collect data","Data presented is the best guess of functional managers","Process participants do not help create the X-Y matrix, FMEA and process map"],answer:0,hint:"A roadblock gets in the way; an approved charter is a normal entry condition.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Measure roadblocks (p. 229) include no time to collect data, data that is the “best guess by functional managers” and process participants left out of the X-Y matrix, FMEA and process map."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"An approved charter is the normal result of Define, not a roadblock."}],final:"An approved charter is not a roadblock → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — lack of time to collect data is a Measure roadblock named by the book.","Incorrect — “best guess” data from functional managers is a Measure roadblock named by the book.","Incorrect — leaving process participants out of these tools is a Measure roadblock named by the book."],bookRef:"Book p. 229"},{id:"sv-1-511",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A billing team runs its new automated tariff check in **one region for four weeks** and compares dispute rates before and after. In which DMAIC phase is this deliverable produced?",options:["Analyze","Control","Improve","Measure"],answer:2,hint:"Testing a solution on a small scale before full roll-out.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"A **pilot** tests a selected solution on a small scale to confirm it works and reduce risk. Pilot results are an **Improve** deliverable."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Analyze proves causes, Measure sets the baseline, Control sustains the gain after full implementation."}],final:"Pilot of a solution = Improve → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Analyze verifies root causes; it does not test solutions in the live process.","Incorrect — Control sustains a solution that has already been implemented, using the control plan.","","Incorrect — Measure establishes the baseline and validates data before any solution exists."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.3"},{id:"sv-1-512",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:1,stem:"A new ticket-routing method is running and control charts are in place. The Process Owner asks: “What will stop the old way of working from coming back after the project team leaves?” Which deliverable answers this?",options:["A revised project charter with a new goal statement","A Gage R&R study on the ticket-timing system","An X-Y matrix of the remaining potential inputs","A control plan with training, documentation, monitoring and response plans"],answer:3,hint:"The Control checklist asks how the process owner will sustain the gain and avoid “reinventing” the old process.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The **control plan**, handed to the process owner, holds the training, documentation, monitoring and response plans that keep the gain in place (pp. 499, 578)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A charter frames a project. Gage R&R validates data in Measure. An X-Y matrix prioritises inputs in Measure."}],final:"Sustaining the gain = control plan handed to the process owner → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the charter frames the project in Define; it does not sustain a running process.","Incorrect — Gage R&R checks the measurement system in Measure; it does not hold the gain.","Incorrect — an X-Y matrix prioritises potential inputs in Measure, not after implementation.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 499, 578"},{id:"sv-1-513",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:3,stem:"In Analyze, a team finds that average restoration time differs between two shifts (**p = 0.03**), but the difference is only 2 minutes against an 8-hour SLA. Following the book’s Analyze roadmap, which question must still be answered before declaring a root cause?",options:["Is the difference practically significant?","Has the project charter been signed?","Is the control plan in place?","Has the financial impact been verified?"],answer:0,hint:"Roadmap: statistically significant? → practically significant? → root cause?",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s Analyze roadmap (p. 238) asks “Statistically significant?” then “**Practically significant?**” before “Root cause?”."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"p = 0.03 shows statistical significance, but 2 minutes on an 8-hour SLA may not matter in practice. That must be judged before calling shift a root cause."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The charter is a Define item; the control plan and financial verification come in Control."}],final:"Statistically significant → still check practical significance → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — charter sign-off belongs to Define and is already complete when Analyze is reached.","Incorrect — the control plan is a Control deliverable, produced after solutions are implemented.","Incorrect — financial impact is verified at the end of Control, not before naming a root cause."],bookRef:"Book p. 238"},{id:"sv-1-514",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A Green Belt has validated the measurement system, shown the process is stable and estimated its capability and sigma level. According to the book’s Measure roadmap, what is the **last** step before the project is “Ready for Analyze”?",options:["Verify the financial impact with Finance","Pilot the chosen solution in one area","Review progress with the Champion","Write the first draft of the problem statement"],answer:2,hint:"Every phase closes with the sponsor looking at the evidence.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Measure roadmap (p. 230) ends: estimate process sigma level → **review progress with Champion** → ready for Analyze."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Financial verification closes Control. Pilots are Improve. The problem statement is written at the start (Define, then made detailed at the start of Measure)."}],final:"Last Measure step = review progress with the Champion → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — verifying financial impact is the final step of Control.","Incorrect — piloting a solution happens in Improve, after root causes are verified.","","Incorrect — the problem statement is written at the start; the Measure roadmap begins with the detailed version."],bookRef:"Book p. 230"},{id:"sv-1-515",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A charter states the problem as: “Fault repairs are slow **because engineers lack training**.” The Champion returns it before the Define tollgate. What is the main reason?",options:["The problem statement must name the person responsible for the delay","The problem statement includes an assumed cause, which has not been verified","The problem statement should also describe the solution to be implemented","A charter should not contain a problem statement until Analyze is complete"],answer:1,hint:"A Define problem statement describes what, where, when and how big — no cause, no solution.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The problem statement bounds and describes the problem (glossary p. 587). It must **not** assume a cause or a solution; causes are verified in Analyze."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"“Because engineers lack training” is an unproven cause, so the statement is not acceptable yet."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Blaming a person, adding a solution or delaying the statement to Analyze all break Define rules."}],final:"Problem statement must not contain an assumed cause → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — naming a culprit adds blame, not a better problem description.","","Incorrect — adding a solution makes it worse; Define problem statements contain no solution.","Incorrect — the problem statement is a core Define deliverable inside the charter."],bookRef:"Book p. 587"},{id:"sv-1-516",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"At the start of Measure, a Green Belt plans to calculate the capability of call-handling time straight away, using an untested time-stamping system. According to the Measure roadmap, what should come **before** the stability and capability assessments?",options:["Verifying the financial impact","Implementing the control plan","Selecting the solution to pilot","Assessing the measurement system"],answer:3,hint:"You cannot judge a process with numbers you cannot trust.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Measure roadmap (p. 230): … select vital few X’s → **assess measurement system** → assess stability → assess capability → estimate sigma level."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The time-stamping system must be validated first; otherwise the capability figure may reflect measurement error, not the process."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Financial verification and control plans are Control steps; solution selection is Improve."}],final:"Measurement system before stability and capability → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — financial impact is verified at the end of Control, not at the start of Measure.","Incorrect — the control plan is implemented in Control, after the solution is chosen.","Incorrect — solutions are selected in Improve, after root causes are verified.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 230"},{id:"sv-1-517",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"At the Improve tollgate, a team presents a pilot for a solution that targets an X it “just knew” was the cause; the X was never tested in Analyze. The Champion sends the team back to verify that X with data. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because solutions must address root causes that have been verified with data in Analyze","Yes, because every Improve tollgate requires a designed experiment","No, because the team’s experience is sufficient evidence of a root cause","No, because a tollgate review cannot send a project back to an earlier phase"],answer:0,hint:"Recycle is a valid tollgate decision; Improve acts on verified causes only.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Root causes are **verified in Analyze**; Improve selects solutions for those verified X’s. A tollgate may **recycle** a project to close a gap."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The solution targets an unverified X, so the evidence chain is broken. Sending the team back is right."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"DOE is used only as necessary. Experience is a hypothesis, not proof. “Recycle” is a normal tollgate outcome."}],final:"Appropriate — Improve must act on verified root causes → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the book says designed experiments are used only as necessary, not at every tollgate.","Incorrect — experience gives a potential cause; proof with data is required in Analyze.","Incorrect — recycling to fix gaps is one of the three standard tollgate decisions."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.3"},{id:"sv-1-518",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"Measure data show the problem is far **smaller** than the charter estimated, and the remaining COPQ no longer justifies the project. What is the BEST action at the Measure tollgate?",options:["Continue to Analyze, because a project cannot be stopped after Define","Present the evidence and let the Champion decide whether to stop the project","Adjust the baseline upward so that the original business case still holds","Skip to Improve so that the team can deliver a quick solution"],answer:1,hint:"Stop is a valid tollgate decision when the evidence no longer supports the project.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Tollgates are evidence-based decisions: go, recycle or **stop**. Stopping a project that is no longer worthwhile is a valid result."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The Belt should present the updated baseline and COPQ honestly, and the Champion decides."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Projects can be stopped at any tollgate. Altering data is unethical. Skipping phases leads to solution jumping."}],final:"Present the evidence; the Champion may stop the project → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — “stop” is a valid tollgate decision at any phase, including Measure.","","Incorrect — changing the baseline to fit the business case falsifies data and destroys trust.","Incorrect — skipping Analyze is solution jumping and spends effort on a problem that may not be worth it."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.3"},{id:"sv-1-519",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"In Analyze, a Green Belt discovers that the “data” for several X’s are estimates written by functional managers, not recorded values. What is the BEST action?",options:["Use the managers’ estimates, because they know the process best","Average the estimates of several managers to remove the bias","Collect real process data, involving process participants in planning the analysis","Skip the hypothesis tests and select the solution most managers prefer"],answer:2,hint:"The book lists “best guess by functional managers” and excluded participants as roadblocks.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s Analyze roadblocks (p. 416) include data that is “the best guess by functional managers” and process participants who do not take part in analysis planning."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The fix is to **collect actual data** and involve the people who do the work."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Estimates, averaged or not, are still opinions. Choosing the favourite solution skips the proof that Analyze requires."}],final:"Replace guesses with real data, with participants involved → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — managers’ guesses are opinions; the book names them as a roadblock, not as evidence.","Incorrect — averaging guesses still produces a guess; it does not turn opinion into data.","","Incorrect — skipping the tests replaces Analyze with opinion and risks fixing the wrong X."],bookRef:"Book p. 416"},{id:"sv-1-520",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"During Measure, a Green Belt finds that failed activations cost far more than the Define estimate. She updates the COPQ and problem statement in the charter and informs the Champion. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because the charter should be rewritten from scratch at every tollgate","Yes, because the Measure checklist asks whether the problem statement has changed and whether more COPQ has been found","No, because the charter is frozen once the Define tollgate is passed","No, because COPQ may be changed only by Finance at project closure"],answer:1,hint:"Problem statement and COPQ are living outputs, revisited in Measure.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Measure checklist (p. 231) asks “Has the Problem Statement changed?” and “Have you identified more COPQ?”. Define outputs are **living documents**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Updating the figures with better data and telling the Champion is exactly what the checklist expects."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Rewriting from scratch is unnecessary. The charter is not frozen. Finance verifies realised savings at closure, but the Belt updates estimates as data arrive."}],final:"Appropriate — the Measure checklist expects updated problem statement and COPQ → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the charter is updated with new evidence, not rewritten from scratch at every tollgate.","","Incorrect — the book’s Measure checklist shows the problem statement and COPQ can change after Define.","Incorrect — Finance verifies realised savings at closure, but estimates are updated by the Belt as data arrive."],bookRef:"Book p. 231"},{id:"sv-1-521",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Green Belt brings this checklist to the **Define** tollgate:

\`\`\`
Define tollgate checklist — Billing disputes
Item                           Status
Project charter (signed)       Complete
VOC translated into CTQs       Complete
Preliminary COPQ estimate      Complete
Stakeholder analysis           Not started
Root cause verified            Complete
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is correct?`,options:["The pack has a Define gap (stakeholder analysis) and an item that belongs to Analyze (verified root cause)","The pack is ready, because only one item is incomplete","The pack is not ready, because COPQ must be verified rather than estimated in Define","The pack is not ready, because a control plan is missing"],answer:0,hint:"Check every row: is it a Define deliverable, and is it complete?",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Charter, CTQs and preliminary COPQ are complete Define items. Stakeholder analysis — also a Define deliverable — has not started. “Root cause verified” is an Analyze deliverable that cannot honestly be complete in Define."},{title:"Decide",body:"The pack has one Define gap and one misplaced claim."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Missing items matter, COPQ is only estimated in Define, and control plans belong to Control."}],final:"Missing stakeholder analysis + premature “verified root cause” → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a missing Define deliverable means the pack is incomplete, and the root-cause row is misplaced.","Incorrect — COPQ is correctly an estimate in Define; it is verified as financial impact in Control.","Incorrect — a control plan is a Control deliverable and is not expected at the Define tollgate."],bookRef:"Syllabus 1.1.3"},{id:"sv-1-522",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:1,stem:`A project tracker shows where each deliverable was recorded:

\`\`\`
Deliverable                  Phase recorded
Data collection plan         Measure
Hypothesis test results      Analyze
Pilot results                Improve
Gage R&R study               Control
\`\`\`

Which entry is recorded in the **wrong** phase?`,options:["Data collection plan","Gage R&R study","Hypothesis test results","Pilot results"],answer:1,hint:"Ask of each row: which phase question does this deliverable answer?",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Data collection plan → Measure ✔. Hypothesis tests → Analyze ✔. Pilot results → Improve ✔."},{title:"Decide",body:"A **Gage R&R study** validates the measurement system, a **Measure** deliverable, so recording it under Control is wrong."}],final:"Gage R&R belongs to Measure, not Control → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a data collection plan is correctly recorded as a Measure deliverable.","","Incorrect — hypothesis test results are correctly recorded as Analyze deliverables.","Incorrect — pilot results are correctly recorded as Improve deliverables."],bookRef:"Book p. 229"},{id:"sv-1-523",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A Green Belt presents this close-out summary:

\`\`\`
Project close-out summary — Fault repair
Primary metric (repair time)   Baseline 7.4 h -> Current 4.1 h
Control plan                   Handed to process owner: Yes
FMEA updated                   Yes
Financial impact               Estimated: 410k   Verified by Finance: No
\`\`\`

Which conclusion is correct?`,options:["The project can close now, because the primary metric has improved","The project must return to Measure, because the FMEA was updated","The project must return to Define, because the control plan was handed over too early","The project cannot close until the financial impact is verified"],answer:3,hint:"Last two steps of the Control roadmap: verify financial impact → go to next project.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"The metric improved, the control plan is with the process owner and the FMEA is updated. Only the financial impact is still an estimate."},{title:"Decide",body:"The Control roadmap ends with **Verify Financial Impact** before “Go to Next Project”, and the checklist asks the Champion, Belt and Finance to agree completion (pp. 499, 578)."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"An improved metric alone does not close a project. Updating the FMEA and handing over the control plan are expected Control actions."}],final:"Not ready — financial impact still unverified → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — an improved metric is not enough; the financial impact must be verified before closure.","Incorrect — updating the FMEA is an expected Control checklist item, not a reason to go back.","Incorrect — handing the control plan to the process owner is exactly what Control requires.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 499, 578"},{id:"sv-1-524",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:3,stem:"The book states that over 80% of projects will realise their solutions in the **Improve** phase through designed experiments. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:1,hint:"Recall the book’s claim about which phase most often reveals the solution.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 417) says “over 80% of projects will realize their solutions in the **Analyze** Phase”. It also says most problems will not need designed experiments (p. 489)."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The statement names Improve and DOE, so it is false."}],final:"Over 80% find solutions in Analyze → False → answer **False**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the book names the Analyze phase, and it says most problems do not need designed experiments.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 417, 489"},{id:"sv-1-525",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:3,stem:`Put these steps of the book’s **Analyze roadmap** in order:

- **P** Perform statistical tests
- **Q** State practical theories
- **R** Collect data
- **S** Translate practical theories into scientific hypotheses`,options:["Q → S → P → R","S → Q → R → P","Q → S → R → P","Q → R → S → P"],answer:2,hint:"Theory → hypothesis → (tools) → data → test.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The Analyze roadmap (p. 238): vital few X’s identified → **state practical theories** → **translate them into scientific hypotheses** → select analysis tools → **collect data** → **perform statistical tests** → statistically and practically significant? → root cause."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Q → S → R → P."}],final:"Q → S → R → P → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — tests cannot be performed before the data are collected.","Incorrect — a practical theory must be stated before it can be translated into a scientific hypothesis.","","Incorrect — the hypothesis is formed before data collection, so the data serve a planned test."],bookRef:"Book p. 238"},{id:"sv-1-526",phase:1,topic:"1.1.3",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`After “Improvement Selected”, put these steps of the book’s **Control roadmap** in order:

- **P** Develop and implement the response plan
- **Q** Develop and implement the training plan
- **R** Verify financial impact
- **S** Align systems and structures`,options:["Q → S → P → R","Q → P → S → R","P → Q → S → R","Q → P → R → S"],answer:1,hint:"Training → documentation → monitoring → response → align systems → verify money → next project.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s “Control Phase Finality” sequence (p. 499): training plan → documentation plan → monitoring plan → **response plan** → plan to align systems and structures → **align systems and structures** → **verify financial impact** → go to next project."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Q → P → S → R."}],final:"Q → P → S → R → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — systems and structures are aligned after the training, documentation, monitoring and response plans.","","Incorrect — the training plan comes first; the response plan follows the monitoring plan.","Incorrect — financial impact is verified last, after systems and structures are aligned."],bookRef:"Book p. 499"},{id:"sv-1-527",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"In the book’s Y = f(X) vocabulary table, Y is called the “**symptom**”. Which word is used for the X on the same row?",options:["Effect","Monitor","Output","Problem"],answer:3,hint:"Book table pairs: dependent/independent, output/input, effect/cause, symptom/problem, monitor/control.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s table (p. 526): Y = dependent, output, effect, **symptom**, monitor; X = independent, input, cause, **problem**, control."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The X counterpart of “symptom” is “problem”: what we see in Y is a symptom of a problem in the X’s."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Effect, monitor and output are all Y-side words in the same table."}],final:"Symptom (Y) ↔ problem (X) → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — “effect” is a Y-side word; its X counterpart is “cause”.","Incorrect — “monitor” is a Y-side word; its X counterpart is “control”.","Incorrect — “output” is a Y-side word; its X counterpart is “input”.",""],trap:"Every word on the Y side has a partner on the X side — symptom pairs with problem.",bookRef:"Book p. 526"},{id:"sv-1-528",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:1,stem:"How does the book’s glossary define a **critical X**?",options:["An input that exerts a significant influence on any one or all of the key outputs","An output that is critical to the customer’s satisfaction","An input that cannot be controlled because it is too costly","Any input that appears on the detailed process map"],answer:0,hint:"Critical X = an input with significant influence on key outputs.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary (p. 585): a critical X is “an input to a process or system that exerts a significant influence on any one or all of the key outputs of a process”."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"An output critical to the customer is a CTQ (a Y). An input too costly to control is a noise input. Every input on the map is only a potential X."}],final:"Critical X = input with significant influence on key outputs → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — an output critical to the customer is a CTQ, which is a Y, not an X.","Incorrect — an input that is too costly or difficult to control is a noise (N) input.","Incorrect — inputs on the process map are potential X’s; only some prove to be critical."],bookRef:"Book p. 585"},{id:"sv-1-529",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:2,stem:"The book’s glossary lists words that are **synonymous with an input**. Which set is it?",options:["Y, CTQ and output","Effect, symptom and monitor","Noise, defect and error term","X, characteristic and input variable"],answer:3,hint:"Input = X. Output = Y (see also CTQ).",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary (p. 586) defines an input as a resource consumed, used or added to a process, “synonymous with **X, characteristic, and input variable**”. The output entry says “see also Y, CTQ”."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Y, CTQ and output are output words. Effect, symptom and monitor are Y-side words from the p. 526 table. Noise is only one class of input, and defects and error terms are not inputs."}],final:"Input = X = characteristic = input variable → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Y, CTQ and output are the glossary’s words for the output, not the input.","Incorrect — effect, symptom and monitor are Y-side words in the book’s Y = f(X) table.","Incorrect — noise is only one class of input, and defects and the error term are not inputs.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 586–587"},{id:"sv-1-530",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"concept",difficulty:3,stem:"Which statement about a **Significant Y**, as defined in the book’s glossary, is CORRECT?",options:["It is an output that exerts a significant influence on the success of the process or the customer","It is an input that exerts a significant influence on the key outputs","It is any output that is cheap and easy to measure","It is the part of the output left unexplained after the X’s are fitted"],answer:0,hint:"Critical X = an important input. Significant Y = an important output.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The glossary (p. 588) defines a Significant Y as “an output of a process that exerts a significant influence on the success of the process or the customer”."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"An input with significant influence on key outputs is a **critical X** (p. 585). Ease of measurement does not make an output significant. Unexplained variation is the error term ε."}],final:"Significant Y = an output that strongly affects process success or the customer → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — an input with significant influence on key outputs is the glossary’s critical X, not a Significant Y.","Incorrect — significance comes from impact on the process or customer, not from how easy the output is to measure.","Incorrect — the unexplained part of Y is the error term ε, not a Significant Y."],trap:"Critical X (input) and Significant Y (output) are look-alike glossary terms.",bookRef:"Book pp. 585, 588"},{id:"sv-1-531",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"statements",difficulty:1,stem:`Which statements about **Y** and **X** in Y = f(X) are CORRECT?

1. Y is the dependent variable.
2. X is the effect.
3. Y should be controlled and X should be monitored.
4. X is the input.`,options:["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],answer:3,hint:"Y: dependent, output, effect, monitor. X: independent, input, cause, control.",steps:[{title:"Check each statement",body:"1 is true. 2 is false — X is the cause; Y is the effect. 3 is false — it is reversed: **monitor Y, control X**. 4 is true."},{title:"Match a pair",body:"The true statements are 1 and 4."}],final:"True statements are 1 and 4 → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — includes statement 2; X is the cause and Y is the effect.","Incorrect — both are false: X is the cause, and the book says monitor Y and control X.","Incorrect — includes statement 3, which reverses the book’s rule of monitoring Y and controlling X.",""],bookRef:"Book p. 526"},{id:"sv-1-532",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"missing-word",difficulty:2,stem:`Identify the missing word in the following sentence.

The book says that if an input is a critical X, then, by definition, you must [?] it.`,options:["control","ignore","inspect","monitor"],answer:0,hint:"Y is monitored; critical X’s are …",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 114): “if it is a Critical X, by definition, you must **control** it” — and anything you control needs a requirement and a measurement."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"“Monitor” is the word the book uses for Y. Inspection checks results after the fact. A critical X can never be ignored."}],final:"Critical X → must be controlled → answer **A**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — a critical X has significant influence on Y, so ignoring it guarantees the problem continues.","Incorrect — inspection checks output after the fact; the book says critical X’s must be controlled.","Incorrect — “monitor” is the book’s word for the Y; critical X’s must be controlled."],bookRef:"Book p. 114"},{id:"sv-1-533",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"not-except",difficulty:1,stem:"Which word is NOT used for the **Y** in the book’s Y = f(X) table?",options:["Dependent","Effect","Independent","Monitor"],answer:2,hint:"One of these words belongs on the X side of the table.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s table (p. 526) describes Y as dependent, output, effect, symptom and monitor."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"“Independent” describes the X’s — the inputs that drive Y."}],final:"“Independent” describes X, not Y → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — Y is the dependent variable; its value depends on the X’s.","Incorrect — Y is the effect produced by the X’s (the causes).","","Incorrect — the book says Y is monitored while the X’s are controlled."],bookRef:"Book p. 526"},{id:"sv-1-534",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"scenario",difficulty:2,stem:"A contact-centre team runs a designed experiment on knowledge-base use and call routing, and ends with an equation that predicts first-contact resolution from those two inputs. In book terms, what has the experiment delivered?",options:["A list of potential X’s for the fishbone diagram","A measurement system analysis of first-contact resolution","The cause-and-effect relationship between the X’s and the Y as a function Y = f(x)","A control chart showing whether the Y is stable"],answer:2,hint:"The book says the end result of an experiment is a mathematical function.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 476) says the end result of experiments is “a mathematical function. Y = f (x)”, giving the true cause-and-effect relationship between the X variables and the Y."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"An equation predicting the Y from the two inputs is exactly that function."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"A fishbone lists potential X’s before any data. MSA checks the measurement system. A control chart shows stability over time."}],final:"Experiment result = Y = f(x), the cause-and-effect relationship → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — a fishbone lists potential X’s from brainstorming; the experiment has gone further and quantified them.","Incorrect — MSA checks whether the measurement system can be trusted; it does not relate X’s to Y.","","Incorrect — a control chart shows stability of one variable over time, not how X’s drive Y."],bookRef:"Book p. 476"},{id:"sv-1-535",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:3,stem:"A team has proven that **order-entry accuracy** is a critical X for activation lead time. The Green Belt sets a requirement of at least 99% accuracy and starts measuring it daily. Was this an appropriate action, and why?",options:["Yes, because once a critical X is found, the Y no longer needs to be measured","Yes, because controlling an input implies a requirement on it and a need to measure it","No, because critical X’s should be monitored only after the Y goes out of specification","No, because only the Y should carry a requirement in a Six Sigma project"],answer:1,hint:"Book p. 114: if you control an input, requirements are implied and it must be measured.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book (p. 114): a critical X must be controlled, and “if you believe an input is or needs to be controlled then… there are requirements placed on it and it must be measured”."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Setting a 99% requirement and measuring it daily puts the critical X under control. The Y is still monitored as the scoreboard."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The Y is still monitored, X’s are controlled before Y fails, and inputs carry requirements too (RUMBA applies to them)."}],final:"Appropriate — a controlled X needs a requirement and a measurement → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the Y is still monitored as the scoreboard; controlling X does not remove the need to watch Y.","","Incorrect — waiting for Y to fail is reactive; the critical X is controlled so Y stays in specification.","Incorrect — the book says controlled inputs carry requirements too, and the same RUMBA rules apply."],bookRef:"Book p. 114"},{id:"sv-1-536",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"best-action",difficulty:2,stem:"A dashboard shows that the share of bills delivered on time (the Y) dropped from 97% to 89% this month. The critical X’s are known and measured. What is the BEST next action?",options:["Ask the billing staff to work faster until the Y recovers","Check the critical X’s to find which input has changed","Lower the Y target to 89% so the dashboard turns green","Add a final inspection of every bill before it is posted"],answer:1,hint:"Y is the symptom; the problem lives in the X’s.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"In Y = f(X), Y is the **symptom** and X is the **problem** (book p. 526). A change in Y means something in the X’s changed."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Because the critical X’s are already measured, the fastest route to the cause is to see which of them moved."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"Pressure on Y, a lower target or more inspection all act on the symptom and leave the cause in place."}],final:"Y moved → check which critical X changed → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — pushing people on the Y treats the symptom and leaves the changed input in place.","","Incorrect — lowering the target hides the problem from the dashboard without fixing it.","Incorrect — inspection catches late bills after the fact; it does not find or fix the changed input."],bookRef:"Book p. 526"},{id:"sv-1-537",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:2,stem:`A team prepares this data collection sheet for fault repair:

\`\`\`
Data collection sheet — Fault repair
Column          Description
RepairTime      hours from ticket open to service restored
EngineerLevel   skill grade (1-3) of the assigned engineer
PartsOnVan      Yes/No, required part already on the van
Rainfall        mm of rain in the region that day
\`\`\`

Which reading of the sheet is consistent with Y = f(X)?`,options:["Rainfall is the Y, because it varies the most from day to day","EngineerLevel is the Y, and RepairTime is a controllable X","All four columns are X’s, because the Y must be customer satisfaction","RepairTime is the Y; EngineerLevel and PartsOnVan are X’s; Rainfall is a noise X"],answer:3,hint:"Which column is the result? Which columns could cause it, and which of those cannot be controlled?",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"RepairTime is the outcome the customer feels — the **Y**. EngineerLevel and PartsOnVan are inputs the team can influence — **X’s**. Rainfall also affects repair time but cannot be controlled — a **noise X**."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The Y is chosen by what the project improves, not by how much a column varies. Reversing input and output breaks the cause-and-effect logic."}],final:"RepairTime = Y; engineer level and parts = X; rainfall = noise X → answer **D**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — rainfall is an uncontrollable input (noise X); the amount of variation does not make a column the Y.","Incorrect — this reverses cause and effect; engineer skill is an input and repair time is the output.","Incorrect — repair time is a measurable output of this process, so it can serve as the project Y.",""],bookRef:"Book pp. 113, 526"},{id:"sv-1-538",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"output",difficulty:3,stem:`A Green Belt shows this control plan extract:

\`\`\`
Control plan extract — Activation lead time
Characteristic         Type  Target       Method          Reaction
Activation lead time   Y     <= 48 h      Weekly chart    Review X’s
Order-entry accuracy   X     >= 99%       Daily p chart   Stop and correct
Credit-check queue     X     <= 4 h       I-MR chart      Escalate
\`\`\`

Which statement BEST describes the strategy shown?`,options:["The Y is controlled directly, and the X’s are only reported for information","The critical X’s are controlled with targets and reaction rules, while the Y is monitored","Only the Y needs a target, because the X’s are noise inputs","The X’s are listed because the Y has not yet been defined"],answer:1,hint:"Book p. 526: control the critical X’s in order to control the Y.",steps:[{title:"Read the output",body:"Each X has a target, frequent charting and a direct reaction (“stop and correct”, “escalate”). The Y has a weekly chart whose reaction is to “review X’s”."},{title:"Apply the principle",body:"This is the book’s rule: **monitor Y, control X** — the X’s are steered, the Y is the scoreboard."},{title:"Eliminate look-alikes",body:"The X’s carry targets, so they are not just reported and not noise. The Y is clearly defined with a 48-hour target."}],final:"Control the critical X’s; monitor the Y → answer **B**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — the X’s have targets and immediate reactions; the Y’s reaction is only to review the X’s.","","Incorrect — both X’s carry targets and reaction rules, so they are controlled inputs, not noise.","Incorrect — the Y is defined (activation lead time, 48-hour target) in the first row."],bookRef:"Book p. 526"},{id:"sv-1-539",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"true-false",difficulty:1,stem:"In Y = f(X), the output Y is described as the **effect** and the inputs X as the **causes**. True or False?",options:["True","False"],answer:0,hint:"Recall the effect/cause row of the book’s table.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"The book’s table (p. 526) lists Y as the **effect** and X as the **cause**."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"The statement matches the table, so it is true."}],final:"Y = effect, X = cause → True → answer **True**",whyWrong:["","Incorrect — the book’s table lists Y as the effect and the X’s as the causes."],bookRef:"Book p. 526"},{id:"sv-1-540",phase:1,topic:"1.1.4",qtype:"sequence",difficulty:2,stem:`Using Y = f(X) logic, put these actions in order when a Y misses its target:

- **P** Adjust or restore the critical X
- **Q** Observe the symptom in the Y
- **R** Confirm that the Y has returned to target
- **S** Find which X has changed`,options:["Q → P → S → R","S → Q → P → R","Q → S → P → R","Q → S → R → P"],answer:2,hint:"Symptom (Y) → problem (X) → fix the X → check the Y.",steps:[{title:"Key idea",body:"Y is the monitored symptom and X is the controlled cause (book p. 526). The Y tells you something is wrong; the X’s tell you what; changing the X fixes it; the Y confirms it."},{title:"Apply to the stem",body:"Q → S → P → R."}],final:"Q → S → P → R → answer **C**",whyWrong:["Incorrect — an X cannot be restored before the team knows which X has changed.","Incorrect — the search for the changed X starts because the Y shows a symptom, so Q comes first.","","Incorrect — the Y can only return to target after the critical X has been restored."],bookRef:"Book p. 526"}];export{e as items};
